1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,920
Business letters, love letters, birthday cards, bills and flyers, all of them need to be delivered.

2
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:10,360
Now.

3
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:14,580
Since the first missive was dispatched over 3,000 years ago,

4
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:19,600
dedicated couriers have risked life and limb to convey the message.

5
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:28,720
Now, the story of the mammoth, complex, and sometimes dangerous job of mail delivery.

6
00:00:28,721 --> 00:00:31,040
on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:46,720
In America, the mailman stops by once a day, six days a week.

8
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:52,260
But moving the U.S. mail is a mammoth, 24-hour, seven-day-a-week endeavor.

9
00:00:52,261 --> 00:01:01,260
When a letter is mailed, it joins over 600 million pieces of mail to and from destinations

10
00:01:01,261 --> 00:01:08,708
all over the nation and the world that are handled every day by the U.S.

11
00:01:08,709 --> 00:01:10,260
Postal Service.

12
00:01:10,261 --> 00:01:17,020
That is in excess of 40% of the entire world's mail volume.

13
00:01:17,021 --> 00:01:22,240
Your letter is then transferred by one of the Postal Service's 200,000 motor vehicles

14
00:01:22,241 --> 00:01:29,460
to its first stop, one of 40,000 United States post offices.

15
00:01:29,461 --> 00:01:37,840
It takes a workforce of 800,000 people to keep the mail moving, making the Postal Service

16
00:01:37,841 --> 00:01:43,980
the largest civilian employer in the U.S.

17
00:01:43,981 --> 00:01:49,247
letter along with countless others headed for 128 million addresses in the U.S.

18
00:01:49,248 --> 00:01:49,948
alone

19
00:01:49,949 --> 00:01:56,140
is first fed into the culling machine, which separates out thicker or odd-shaped envelopes

20
00:01:56,141 --> 00:02:00,314
from manual sorting. Then it's on to this machine.

21
00:02:00,315 --> 00:02:03,320
Its function is to cancel the posted

22
00:02:03,321 --> 00:02:08,700
stamps and then sort the letters into groups according to the direction in which the stamp

23
00:02:08,701 --> 00:02:13,400
is facing.

24
00:02:13,401 --> 00:02:16,060
Next, this machine.

25
00:02:16,061 --> 00:02:20,580
It's equipped with a small camera that actually reads the zip code of the

26
00:02:20,581 --> 00:02:22,400
addressee.

27
00:02:22,401 --> 00:02:30,720
That zip code is then sprayed onto the envelope in the form of a barcode.

28
00:02:30,721 --> 00:02:38,320
Then this machine sorts the letters into general zip code destinations.

29
00:02:38,321 --> 00:02:43,100
all the letters for that general destination are then boxed and sealed

30
00:02:43,101 --> 00:02:50,100
a computer designates each box for a specific airline flight

31
00:02:50,101 --> 00:02:54,920
the next stop is the airport where your letter is put on board one of fifteen

32
00:02:54,921 --> 00:02:58,460
thousand postal service and private carrier flights

33
00:02:58,461 --> 00:03:03,280
carrying the u.s. mail each day

34
00:03:03,281 --> 00:03:07,000
upon arrival at its destination your letter and the others are placed into

35
00:03:07,001 --> 00:03:13,660
another sorting machine that reads the barcode, determining to which local post office each

36
00:03:13,661 --> 00:03:15,015
should be sent.

37
00:03:15,016 --> 00:03:21,340
At that local post office, that same type of machine sorts your letter

38
00:03:21,341 --> 00:03:28,740
right down to the delivery route order for the letter carrier who will complete the delivery.

39
00:03:28,741 --> 00:03:32,639
We provide a universal service at a uniform price.

40
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:35,680
Now that means that we deliver to every

41
00:03:35,681 --> 00:03:41,527
address in the United States every day, six days a week, all year long.

42
00:03:41,528 --> 00:03:42,680
We're the only

43
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:48,710
company that does that. But where did this amazing process begin?

44
00:03:48,711 --> 00:03:50,480
What are today's roots

45
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,432
of the postal service?

46
00:03:53,433 --> 00:03:58,740
The first form of writing we know of is the 5,000-year-old symbols of

47
00:03:58,741 --> 00:04:05,740
The cuneiform of writing we know of is the 5,000 year old symbols of the Sumerians of Mesopotamia.

48
00:04:05,741 --> 00:04:15,740
This basic written communication called cuneiform evolved into a phonetic alphabet by about 2000 BC.

49
00:04:15,741 --> 00:04:20,740
With this advancement more complex ideas could be conveyed,

50
00:04:20,741 --> 00:04:27,740
and the many peoples of Mesopotamia began to adopt the method of writing correspondences on clay tablets,

51
00:04:27,741 --> 00:04:30,700
which were sent by runners to their destinations.

52
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:37,260
These runners are the earliest known postal messengers.

53
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:41,120
Such a method was of course not secure

54
00:04:41,121 --> 00:04:44,320
in cases where secret messages needed to be delivered.

55
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:49,020
Legend tells of one rather unusual method

56
00:04:49,021 --> 00:04:53,080
credited to Persian royalty in the mid-6th century B.C.

57
00:04:53,081 --> 00:05:00,780
A sender of a letter would take one of his slaves and shave his head and tattoo the message

58
00:05:00,781 --> 00:05:07,040
into his head and hold him in communicado until his hair grew out and then whisper in his ear,

59
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:10,482
it's to go to Joe's. And then the messenger would go to Joe's.

60
00:05:10,483 --> 00:05:12,260
At that point, they'd shave his head

61
00:05:12,261 --> 00:05:13,777
and read the message.

62
00:05:13,778 --> 00:05:18,980
The well-maintained paved roads of ancient Rome were built primarily for

63
00:05:18,981 --> 00:05:23,060
the movement of military troops and messages.

64
00:05:23,061 --> 00:05:29,480
But ancient government postal services were not for use by the populace.

65
00:05:29,481 --> 00:05:34,760
They were reserved for the official business of royalty.

66
00:05:34,761 --> 00:05:42,100
However, in 1100 AD, Kublai Khan created a system in China that lasted 600 years

67
00:05:42,101 --> 00:05:46,540
and was available to all.

68
00:05:46,541 --> 00:05:52,960
A chain of 10,000 post offices served the kingdom, all in communication with each other

69
00:05:52,961 --> 00:05:58,160
via horsemen and boat.

70
00:05:58,161 --> 00:06:03,040
As the Middle Ages came to an end in Europe, most countries maintained some kind of postal

71
00:06:03,041 --> 00:06:07,140
operation.

72
00:06:07,141 --> 00:06:12,700
European monarchs in the 16th century, such as England's King Henry VIII, were so concerned

73
00:06:12,701 --> 00:06:15,640
with the mail being transported properly and rapidly,

74
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:18,840
that they set harsh penalties for failure.

75
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:23,740
If you miscarried the mail, you might forfeit your life,

76
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,140
or you might forfeit a significant percentage of your salary,

77
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:30,360
so much so that your family would perish in the process.

78
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:32,880
In fact, if you look at some European letters,

79
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:34,380
there's a design device.

80
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:37,880
That's actually the stylized image of a gallows

81
00:06:37,881 --> 00:06:40,220
to remind the letter carrier that if he delayed the mail,

82
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:41,400
he forfeited his life.

83
00:06:42,700 --> 00:06:49,700
A century later, another British king broke with tradition and opened the Royal Mail Service

84
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:53,140
to the masses.

85
00:06:53,141 --> 00:06:58,200
King Charles needed some extra revenues and he saw a way of getting those revenues in

86
00:06:58,201 --> 00:07:05,420
by freeing up the post to the general public.

87
00:07:05,421 --> 00:07:10,760
In 1639, the first official post office appeared in British North America.

88
00:07:10,761 --> 00:07:12,420
It was in the Fairbanks

89
00:07:12,421 --> 00:07:17,420
Tavern in Boston, so chosen because it was a common meeting place for the people and

90
00:07:17,421 --> 00:07:22,420
for its location on the wharf, where mail arrived from overseas.

91
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:30,420
Those ships took as long as a month to journey to and from England.

92
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:38,420
Friends, merchants or Native American messengers usually carried unofficial mail within the colonies.

93
00:07:38,421 --> 00:07:45,903
colonies. The colonists established a central postal organization in 1691.

94
00:07:45,904 --> 00:07:47,420
Its rights were

95
00:07:47,421 --> 00:07:52,420
purchased by the crown in 1707 when it was determined that there was a profit to be made

96
00:07:52,421 --> 00:07:59,420
from the operation. John Hamilton was then appointed first deputy postmaster general

97
00:07:59,421 --> 00:08:08,420
of America. Hamilton's most notable achievement may have come in 1736, when the

98
00:08:08,421 --> 00:08:14,280
with the naming of Benjamin Franklin, a 31-year-old struggling printer and publisher,

99
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:17,420
to the position of Postmaster of Philadelphia.

100
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:23,940
Franklin rose to the position of Deputy Postmaster General in 1753.

101
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:28,440
Franklin immediately set out to reorganize the service.

102
00:08:29,380 --> 00:08:33,920
He mapped out new and shorter postal loads, complete with milestone markers.

103
00:08:33,921 --> 00:08:40,920
Also, he scheduled night runs for postal riders between Philadelphia and New York, cutting travel time in half.

104
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:55,920
In the year 1792, there were 75 post offices in the U.S., each serving about 43,000 customers.

105
00:08:55,921 --> 00:08:56,698
customers.

106
00:08:56,699 --> 00:09:02,920
By the early 1800s, postal officials realized that transportation was the key to

107
00:09:02,921 --> 00:09:05,852
mail delivery for a growing nation.

108
00:09:05,853 --> 00:09:10,040
So the post office helped to develop and subsidize

109
00:09:10,041 --> 00:09:14,594
every new mode of transport introduced into the U.S.

110
00:09:14,595 --> 00:09:17,660
Stagecoaches were employed to carry

111
00:09:17,661 --> 00:09:23,280
mail on the better post roads, and steamboats were contracted for mail delivery to places

112
00:09:23,281 --> 00:09:24,640
where no roads existed.

113
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:27,320
In 1792,

114
00:09:27,540 --> 00:09:29,100
a post office really consisted

115
00:09:29,101 --> 00:09:31,260
of little more than a single string of

116
00:09:31,261 --> 00:09:33,560
offices from Maine to Georgia.

117
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:35,040
By 1800,

118
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:36,760
it had become a network of

119
00:09:36,761 --> 00:09:39,820
about 800. By 1831,

120
00:09:40,060 --> 00:09:41,440
you had about 10,000.

121
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:43,900
By 1840,

122
00:09:43,901 --> 00:09:46,320
there were more than 13,000

123
00:09:46,321 --> 00:09:48,020
post offices serving an average

124
00:09:48,021 --> 00:09:49,900
of 1,100 patrons each.

125
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:59,580
The post office's growth and development was of great benefit to the population centers on the East Coast.

126
00:10:00,220 --> 00:10:07,280
However, America was expanding westward, and with the discovery of gold in California in 1849,

127
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,440
the pioneer movement began to boom.

128
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:14,280
It took a hearty, adventurous spirit to brave the move west,

129
00:10:14,281 --> 00:10:19,536
and it was men of that same spirit who were entrusted with getting the U.S.

130
00:10:19,537 --> 00:10:23,040
mail through to the settlers of the new frontiers.

131
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:29,300
In the early 1800s, letter carriers earned no salaries,

132
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:34,000
but were paid two cents by the recipient for each letter they delivered.

133
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:38,240
Mail delivery will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

134
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:43,620
We now return to mail delivery on Modern Marvels.

135
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:49,920
As a result of America's booming westward expansion of the mid-1800s,

136
00:10:49,921 --> 00:10:50,621
the U.S.

137
00:10:50,622 --> 00:10:56,500
Post Office Department employed large ships to carry mail from the east coast to California.

138
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:04,520
The most favored route was down the east coast to the isthmus of Panama,

139
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:10,980
across by Oxcart, and then north by a clipper ship to the west coast.

140
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:15,180
The trip across the isthmus took approximately six weeks.

141
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:21,320
The alternative was a three-month journey around the tip of South America.

142
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:30,080
But there was another way.

143
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:34,300
The Old West was served by many private express companies,

144
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:36,880
some small, some large.

145
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:39,760
But none rivaled Wells Fargo.

146
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:45,020
For all intents and purposes, American Rail and Telegraph Service stopped

147
00:11:45,021 --> 00:11:48,820
at the Missouri River, and so did the U.S. Post Office.

148
00:11:51,060 --> 00:11:55,420
And that was one signal failure of the post office in the 19th century.

149
00:11:55,421 --> 00:12:00,000
The government did a very good job of extending service to Michigan

150
00:12:00,001 --> 00:12:05,140
and to the Southwest, the old Southwest, Arkansas, Texas, and so on.

151
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:08,240
But it had great trouble in California.

152
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:12,760
And it was there that entrepreneurs like Wells and Fargo

153
00:12:12,761 --> 00:12:14,720
really saw their opening.

154
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:20,820
Not only gold miners were making fortunes in California,

155
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:27,800
express companies were commonly charging 3 to 5 percent of the value of a gold shipment to send it back east.

156
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:36,760
The total value of the gold shipped in 1851 alone was $60 million.

157
00:12:36,761 --> 00:12:42,920
dollars. Henry Wells and William Fargo opened their freighting and banking

158
00:12:42,921 --> 00:12:52,260
company in San Francisco in 1852. By 1855 through buyouts consolidation and

159
00:12:52,261 --> 00:13:00,260
good luck Wells Fargo was the only game in town. The post office in the western

160
00:13:00,261 --> 00:13:05,620
reaches of the US was virtually non-existent so Wells Fargo began

161
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:10,020
with private overland mail service to the east.

162
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:15,240
The familiar dark green letterboxes of Wells Fargo

163
00:13:15,241 --> 00:13:18,520
were a common sight on the streets of California towns

164
00:13:18,521 --> 00:13:24,880
and were sometimes brazenly placed directly next to the red U.S. post boxes.

165
00:13:28,340 --> 00:13:33,180
With its 1,500 horses and 150 stagecoaches,

166
00:13:33,181 --> 00:13:37,640
Wells Fargo handled more mail in the West than did the post office.

167
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:45,380
In California, they actually monopolized the letter-carrying business

168
00:13:45,381 --> 00:13:49,100
from the 1840s right up until the 1880s.

169
00:13:49,400 --> 00:13:55,940
If you were living in California, you felt that Wells Fargo was doing a much better job than the post office.

170
00:13:57,620 --> 00:14:02,520
It was even said that in the West, Wells Fargo was the post office.

171
00:14:03,180 --> 00:14:07,620
But even with the best efforts of Wells Fargo and other express companies,

172
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:12,060
mail rarely reached California in fewer than 23 days.

173
00:14:15,140 --> 00:14:18,400
Speed remained the enemy of mail delivery.

174
00:14:22,420 --> 00:14:28,032
In 1860, the border of the western frontier of the U.S. was the Missouri town of St.

175
00:14:28,033 --> 00:14:28,733
Joseph.

176
00:14:28,734 --> 00:14:40,026
In March of that year, the giant freighting firm of Russell, Majors and Waddell set out to prove that a team of couriers on horseback cutting straight through the

177
00:14:40,027 --> 00:14:46,500
heart of the West could beat all other express services' best times and do so convincingly.

178
00:14:46,501 --> 00:14:56,188
They set up a string of 100 relay and home stations for the riders along the nearly 2,000-mile route from St.

179
00:14:56,189 --> 00:15:03,920
Joseph to Sacramento, California, with riverboat service continuing into San Francisco.

180
00:15:03,921 --> 00:15:12,380
Relay stations were every 10 to 15 miles, and they would change horses at every relay station,

181
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:16,520
and then they would change riders at every home station.

182
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:25,160
Generally, home stations were established locations, very likely stagecoach stops,

183
00:15:25,620 --> 00:15:28,840
whereas the relay stations would be far more primitive.

184
00:15:28,841 --> 00:15:36,400
When the rider arrived at a relay station, as soon as he dismounted, he would hop on

185
00:15:36,401 --> 00:15:44,198
a fresh horse and continue. Riders covered as much as 100 miles in a day.

186
00:15:44,199 --> 00:15:45,480
The schedule

187
00:15:45,481 --> 00:15:52,264
allowed for just 10 days to complete a run between San Francisco and Missouri.

188
00:15:52,265 --> 00:15:52,965
The Pony

189
00:15:52,966 --> 00:15:57,680
Express riders beat the best time of the Overland stage by more than half.

190
00:15:57,681 --> 00:16:05,200
There is a famous poster which is quoted as saying, wanted young skinny wiry

191
00:16:05,201 --> 00:16:14,320
fellows not over 18 willing to risk death daily orphans preferred. The pay was

192
00:16:14,321 --> 00:16:22,080
$50 a month. The riders included Bronco Charlie Miller only 11 years old and

193
00:16:22,081 --> 00:16:28,820
and 14-year-old William Cody, later to be known internationally as Buffalo Bill.

194
00:16:32,020 --> 00:16:40,140
They carried nearly 38,000 pieces of mail, a combined distance equal to 24 times around the earth.

195
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:46,880
Just one shipment was ever lost, and only one rider was killed.

196
00:16:48,220 --> 00:16:51,740
Billy Tate, he was killed by Indians.

197
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:54,520
but the horse continued on in.

198
00:16:57,560 --> 00:17:01,320
The riders had the finest horse flesh available.

199
00:17:01,321 --> 00:17:06,720
They did not use sidearms to try and protect themselves,

200
00:17:06,940 --> 00:17:09,480
except if they absolutely had to.

201
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:14,540
They relied on the horse to simply outrun the trouble.

202
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:20,220
Following the closing of the final link

203
00:17:20,221 --> 00:17:22,140
in the transcontinental telegraph,

204
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:24,660
the last rider on the last run

205
00:17:24,661 --> 00:17:26,740
handed over the mail in California

206
00:17:26,741 --> 00:17:29,500
and the Pony Express slipped into legend

207
00:17:29,501 --> 00:17:32,420
just 19 months after it had begun.

208
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:41,260
It was about this time

209
00:17:41,261 --> 00:17:43,500
that the post office's use of trains

210
00:17:43,501 --> 00:17:45,620
