218
00:18:28,740 --> 00:18:33,120
By 1930, more than 10,000 train routes were being used

219
00:18:33,121 --> 00:18:36,620
to move the mail to every big city and hamlet in the U.S.

220
00:18:36,621 --> 00:18:45,420
and you had a team of very highly skilled men they were all men and they sorted the mail

221
00:18:45,421 --> 00:18:52,300
for the entire united states that's why big city post offices were located next to railway stations

222
00:18:52,301 --> 00:18:58,120
it was a moving system it was inconceivable with the stage coaches and it's inconceivable

223
00:18:58,121 --> 00:19:05,851
with airplanes today. The last RPO made its final run on June 30, 1977.

224
00:19:05,852 --> 00:19:08,900
Increasing loss of ridership

225
00:19:08,901 --> 00:19:18,380
over the years to airlines was a major factor in the decision.

226
00:19:18,381 --> 00:19:23,120
Through the early 1800s, postal

227
00:19:23,121 --> 00:19:29,054
customers had the option of having the recipient of the letter pay the postage fee.

228
00:19:29,055 --> 00:19:29,840
The problem

229
00:19:29,841 --> 00:19:35,346
was that the addressee often refused to pay, thus refusing the letter.

230
00:19:35,347 --> 00:19:36,840
In such a case, the

231
00:19:36,841 --> 00:19:40,240
post office's labor and delivery costs were never recovered.

232
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:48,920
In 1840, the British Royal Mail introduced the idea of prepayment of postage with an

233
00:19:48,921 --> 00:19:52,500
adhesive label known as the penny black. The U.S.

234
00:19:52,501 --> 00:19:56,080
followed the British lead and introduced the five

235
00:19:56,081 --> 00:20:02,220
cent Benjamin Franklin stamp and the ten cent George Washington stamp in 1847.

236
00:20:04,300 --> 00:20:10,380
In 1855, prepayment of postage became mandatory in the United States.

237
00:20:12,900 --> 00:20:17,980
Convenience for the postal customer was increased with the appearance of letterboxes on street

238
00:20:17,981 --> 00:20:19,556
corners in 1858.

239
00:20:19,557 --> 00:20:27,240
As the 1800s neared an end, a new postmaster general with insightful ideas for

240
00:20:27,241 --> 00:20:32,527
the future took office. He realized that population growth in the U.S.

241
00:20:32,528 --> 00:20:35,020
and its continuing shift westward

242
00:20:35,021 --> 00:20:39,731
were creating a steadily increasing workload for the post office.

243
00:20:39,732 --> 00:20:41,760
He also realized that people

244
00:20:41,761 --> 00:20:46,760
alone could not be expected to keep up with this tremendous growth.

245
00:20:50,760 --> 00:20:53,760
Modernization was the key.

246
00:20:57,760 --> 00:21:02,760
The Pony Express was successful at delivering the mail, but not at making money.

247
00:21:03,760 --> 00:21:06,760
Its owners, Russell Majors and Waddell, went bankrupt.

248
00:21:06,761 --> 00:21:14,680
mail delivery will return in a moment on modern models we now return to mail

249
00:21:14,681 --> 00:21:23,560
delivery on modern models in 1790 the US mail consisted of about 1,000 letters

250
00:21:23,561 --> 00:21:32,860
per day by 1890 the nation's 150,000 postal workers were handling 8,000

251
00:21:32,861 --> 00:21:39,100
letters per minute.

252
00:21:39,101 --> 00:21:44,160
John Wanamaker became the Postmaster General in 1889.

253
00:21:44,161 --> 00:21:49,240
While he praised the accomplishments of the US Post Office Department in its first 100

254
00:21:49,241 --> 00:21:50,360
years,

255
00:21:50,361 --> 00:21:53,580
he pointed out that the way the mail was handled

256
00:21:53,581 --> 00:22:01,100
had remained virtually unchanged for a century.

257
00:22:01,101 --> 00:22:05,620
Wanamaker's goal to make the mails go faster, more safely, and more frequently

258
00:22:05,621 --> 00:22:09,460
was to dominate the work of the post office during his term.

259
00:22:10,760 --> 00:22:14,920
But Wanamaker had even greater plans for the post office.

260
00:22:15,640 --> 00:22:18,520
He did help to speed the mails.

261
00:22:18,640 --> 00:22:24,180
He was an extraordinarily ambitious man in his business dealings and as postmaster general.

262
00:22:24,980 --> 00:22:28,480
He helped to promote rural free delivery.

263
00:22:28,481 --> 00:22:34,740
Although it did not become available until after Wanamaker left office in 1893,

264
00:22:35,580 --> 00:22:41,400
rural free delivery was finally instituted just before the turn of the 20th century.

265
00:22:42,400 --> 00:22:46,940
More than 50% of America's population lived in farming regions.

266
00:22:48,160 --> 00:22:52,320
Those people, too, now receive their daily mail free of charge,

267
00:22:52,740 --> 00:22:55,820
a service that had been enjoyed in urban areas for decades.

268
00:22:55,821 --> 00:23:00,860
But he was unsuccessful in his three most ambitious ventures.

269
00:23:01,320 --> 00:23:03,700
He wanted the government to take over the telegraph,

270
00:23:03,820 --> 00:23:05,840
he wanted the government to take over the telephone,

271
00:23:06,300 --> 00:23:09,060
and he wanted the government to institute a parcel post.

272
00:23:09,480 --> 00:23:14,680
And in all three of those ventures, he was blocked by powerful business groups.

273
00:23:16,520 --> 00:23:22,960
Prior to 1892, all U.S. postage stamps were basically uniform in design,

274
00:23:22,961 --> 00:23:28,000
mostly featuring the likenesses of Benjamin Franklin and George Washington.

275
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:32,280
Wanamaker decided to try something fresh and new.

276
00:23:33,320 --> 00:23:38,940
To celebrate the 400th anniversary of Christopher Columbus's arrival in the New World,

277
00:23:39,400 --> 00:23:43,360
the post office issued a series of commemorative postage stamps.

278
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:49,780
Congress criticized the idea, but Wanamaker defended the stamps saying

279
00:23:49,781 --> 00:23:52,680
they could become moneymakers for the post office.

280
00:23:52,960 --> 00:23:54,320
He was right.

281
00:23:56,060 --> 00:23:59,800
Today there is a wide interest in commemorative stamps,

282
00:24:00,380 --> 00:24:04,620
sparked in part by the 1993 release of the Elvis Presley stamp.

283
00:24:06,740 --> 00:24:11,660
The commemorative stamps that we do are a benefit to the Postal Service.

284
00:24:12,260 --> 00:24:17,340
We're at $280 million a year on commemorative stamps,

285
00:24:17,920 --> 00:24:20,880
and we consider that's going to continue to grow.

286
00:24:20,881 --> 00:24:27,880
Many of these stamps go right into private collections, much to the delight of the postal service,

287
00:24:27,881 --> 00:24:33,880
which profits from the sale of the stamp but never incurs the cost of delivering a letter.

288
00:24:37,880 --> 00:24:43,880
As early as 1877, the U.S. Post Office began to use machines to cancel stamps,

289
00:24:43,881 --> 00:24:46,880
replacing the manual method.

290
00:24:46,881 --> 00:24:47,581
method.

291
00:24:47,582 --> 00:24:52,980
One of the earliest innovators of canceling machines in this country was Thomas

292
00:24:52,981 --> 00:24:59,060
Levitt from Massachusetts. They had their limitations. They could only cancel postal

293
00:24:59,061 --> 00:25:04,750
cards because of their uniform size, but they were very efficient.

294
00:25:04,751 --> 00:25:06,560
Levitt's hand-cranked

295
00:25:06,561 --> 00:25:10,358
machine could cancel 15,000 postcards in one hour.

296
00:25:10,359 --> 00:25:13,320
The most adept postal worker could only

297
00:25:13,321 --> 00:25:15,820
hoped to hand cancel 2,000 in an hour.

298
00:25:20,240 --> 00:25:23,860
Other companies introduced belt-driven and then electric

299
00:25:23,861 --> 00:25:26,380
canceling machines in the following decades.

300
00:25:28,560 --> 00:25:33,800
By 1920, the post office owned and operated nearly 3,000

301
00:25:33,801 --> 00:25:34,860
canceling machines.

302
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,600
Experiments in automated sorting of mail

303
00:25:39,601 --> 00:25:43,360
began at the main post office in Washington, D.C. in 1907.

304
00:25:45,280 --> 00:25:48,820
These early innovations were not as productive as they should have been.

305
00:25:49,040 --> 00:25:50,260
A lot of things would jam.

306
00:25:51,220 --> 00:25:53,880
They weren't as speedy as you might think.

307
00:25:54,100 --> 00:25:58,220
They relied on somebody sitting there with a keyboard kind of mechanism

308
00:25:58,221 --> 00:26:00,340
that didn't always work.

309
00:26:00,780 --> 00:26:02,340
You know, first steps don't always work.

310
00:26:02,680 --> 00:26:03,840
You trip and you fall.

311
00:26:04,200 --> 00:26:07,160
But in the course of tripping and falling, you learn how to walk.

312
00:26:07,161 --> 00:26:11,160
In this case, walking would come a little later in the 1950s.

313
00:26:11,161 --> 00:26:16,160
We'd really begin to take our first unfettered steps.

314
00:26:19,160 --> 00:26:24,160
Another romantic era in the history of mail delivery was about to dawn.

315
00:26:24,161 --> 00:26:31,160
One that would ultimately capture the attention and fire the imagination of the entire world.

316
00:26:31,161 --> 00:26:31,861
world.

317
00:26:31,862 --> 00:26:41,660
The word for stamp collecting, philately, was coined from a combination of Greek words,

318
00:26:41,661 --> 00:26:47,480
meaning the love of being tax-free, alluding to the fact that before postage stamps, letters

319
00:26:47,481 --> 00:26:54,775
were sent collect. Mail delivery will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

320
00:26:54,776 --> 00:26:56,040
We now return

321
00:26:56,041 --> 00:27:01,040
to mail delivery on modern models.

322
00:27:01,041 --> 00:27:07,160
The next great frontier for the post office was the skies.

323
00:27:07,161 --> 00:27:11,560
A quite unusual method for delivery of mail through the air was proposed in

324
00:27:11,561 --> 00:27:16,320
1801 by a German newspaper editor.

325
00:27:16,321 --> 00:27:23,120
The plan was to fire hollow mortar shells filled with mail

326
00:27:23,121 --> 00:27:27,620
from town to town.

327
00:27:27,621 --> 00:27:32,400
His idea died on the drawing board.

328
00:27:32,401 --> 00:27:36,760
Englishman Captain Walter Windham is credited with organizing the first

329
00:27:36,761 --> 00:27:41,240
official delivery of mail by airplane.

330
00:27:41,241 --> 00:27:46,440
Windham, a motor car producer and racer who was enamored with aviation, sold his

331
00:27:46,441 --> 00:27:49,000
business and booked passage to India

332
00:27:49,001 --> 00:27:51,760
with two pilots and eight airplanes in tow.

333
00:27:53,120 --> 00:27:55,420
Walter Windham was one of those visionaries

334
00:27:55,421 --> 00:27:59,000
that appeared at the early days of aviation.

335
00:27:59,720 --> 00:28:02,800
A man who could see the future,

336
00:28:03,040 --> 00:28:05,760
and he threw his life and his fortune

337
00:28:05,761 --> 00:28:08,960
into promoting the interests of aviation.

338
00:28:10,480 --> 00:28:13,360
A clergyman who hoped to raise money

339
00:28:13,361 --> 00:28:16,000
to build a new hostel approached Captain Windham

340
00:28:16,001 --> 00:28:21,100
and suggested that by inaugurating an aerial post, funds could be raised.

341
00:28:21,900 --> 00:28:25,740
The challenge was perfectly suited for the ambitious aviator,

342
00:28:25,741 --> 00:28:31,980
and the world's first airmail flight took off on February 18, 1911.

343
00:28:38,080 --> 00:28:44,340
Just seven months later, the U.S. post office's first experimental airmail flight

344
00:28:44,341 --> 00:28:46,300
took off from Long Island, New York.

345
00:28:48,980 --> 00:28:52,380
There were more experimental flights in the following years,

346
00:28:52,660 --> 00:28:55,100
but Congressional support was slow in coming.

347
00:28:56,680 --> 00:29:00,860
In 1918, Congress appropriated the needed $100,000

348
00:29:00,861 --> 00:29:03,580
for the first official airmail routes,

349
00:29:04,140 --> 00:29:07,240
using pilots and planes supplied by the Army.

350
00:29:09,420 --> 00:29:12,840
The pilots called those first planes flying coffins

351
00:29:12,841 --> 00:29:16,440
because of the dangers involved with flying them.

352
00:29:17,300 --> 00:29:20,840
The instrumentation that the early airmail pilots had

353
00:29:20,841 --> 00:29:23,440
were basically an unreliable compass,

354
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:27,080
an airspeed meter, an altimeter,

355
00:29:27,480 --> 00:29:30,460
and a revolution counter for the engine.

356
00:29:31,540 --> 00:29:33,200
It is nothing short of a miracle

357
00:29:33,201 --> 00:29:36,200
that they were able to perform at the level they did

358
00:29:36,201 --> 00:29:37,900
given these crude instruments.

359
00:29:39,080 --> 00:29:41,260
Thousands of spectators converged

360
00:29:41,261 --> 00:29:49,200
on Washington, D.C.'s Potomac Park on May 15, 1918, to witness the historic first official

361
00:29:49,201 --> 00:29:56,640
postal flight to New York City by way of Philadelphia.

362
00:29:56,641 --> 00:30:01,720
President Woodrow Wilson presented engraved watches as commemorative gifts for the pilots

363
00:30:01,721 --> 00:30:05,920
flying the various legs of the route.

364
00:30:05,921 --> 00:30:11,660
workers loaded mailbags into pilot Lieutenant George Boyle's plane and he took off into the

365
00:30:11,661 --> 00:30:16,033
late morning sky to the cheers of the crowd.

366
00:30:16,034 --> 00:30:21,300
For nine years, the post office operated the flights.

367
00:30:21,860 --> 00:30:27,920
In that period, out of the roughly 400 pilots that flew the mail, 34 died in crashes,

368
00:30:28,760 --> 00:30:35,440
mostly as a result of bad weather. It should be noted that at this time,

369
00:30:35,441 --> 00:30:37,502
pilots did not wear parachutes.

370
00:30:37,503 --> 00:30:41,360
Pilots voted against wearing them in large part because it

371
00:30:41,361 --> 00:30:43,199
was their job to save the mail.

372
00:30:43,200 --> 00:30:46,640
And if they jumped out of the airplane, the mail was lost.

373
00:30:48,240 --> 00:30:54,960
America's new love affair with aviation turned to obsession with the first transatlantic flight,

374
00:30:54,961 --> 00:31:00,720
New York to Paris by former airmail pilot Charles Lindbergh in May of 1927.

375
00:31:00,721 --> 00:31:07,720
But Lindbergh knew that he alone did not deserve the world's adulation.

376
00:31:07,721 --> 00:31:13,720
Lindbergh once said that he provided the match to the bonfire

377
00:31:13,721 --> 00:31:19,720
and that people tended to mistake the light of the match for the light of the bonfire.

378
00:31:19,721 --> 00:31:25,720
And I think that's true. The bonfire was the pioneering work of the airmail service.

379
00:31:30,720 --> 00:31:40,200
The Post Office Department closed down its airmail operations in 1927.

380
00:31:41,140 --> 00:31:46,040
Private air carriers were awarded government contracts to carry the U.S. mail.

381
00:31:46,980 --> 00:31:49,700
Such was the beginning of commercial aviation.

382
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:56,640
To this day, all regular U.S. mail is flown under contract by private airlines.

383
00:31:56,641 --> 00:32:03,640
The Postal Service does, though, maintain a fleet of planes to fly shipments of urgent delivery mail.

384
00:32:03,641 --> 00:32:17,641
Yesterday, December 7th, 1941, a date which will live in infamy.

385
00:32:21,640 --> 00:32:22,340
me.

386
00:32:22,341 --> 00:32:32,360
When America entered World War II, one of the post office's most important concerns

387
00:32:32,361 --> 00:32:41,417
was providing mail service to the armed forces scattered over the globe. The U.S.

388
00:32:41,418 --> 00:32:42,200
War and

389
00:32:42,201 --> 00:32:47,040
Navy departments encouraged letter writing between service personnel overseas and loved

390
00:32:47,041 --> 00:32:53,640
ones back home, stating that frequent and rapid communication strengthens fortitude

391
00:32:53,641 --> 00:32:57,960
and livens patriotism and makes loneliness endurable.

392
00:33:00,540 --> 00:33:06,620
To cut down on the tremendous amount of space taken up on military planes and ships by sacks

393
00:33:06,621 --> 00:33:12,960
of mail to and from service personnel, V-mail was introduced in June of 1942.

394
00:33:12,961 --> 00:33:17,280
V standing for victory.

395
00:33:19,340 --> 00:33:24,160
The post office encouraged that all letters to and from those in the military overseas

396
00:33:24,161 --> 00:33:29,420
be written on special letter forms that were then photographed onto microfilm.

397
00:33:31,280 --> 00:33:36,640
The film, not the bulky letters, was then sent overseas to military post offices.

398
00:33:36,641 --> 00:33:44,980
183,000 microfilm letters could travel in a container the size of a hat box

399
00:33:44,981 --> 00:33:53,240
at that point microfilm would be used to produce a small photographic replica of the letter and

400
00:33:53,241 --> 00:33:58,220
that would be what the service man or service woman would receive out in the field it saved

401
00:33:58,221 --> 00:34:05,420
tremendous volumes of mail it was not an american idea it was actually a british idea the idea was

402
00:34:05,421 --> 00:34:07,340
brilliance, absolute brilliance.

403
00:34:08,600 --> 00:34:09,540
More than

404
00:34:09,541 --> 00:34:11,000
one and a quarter million

405
00:34:11,001 --> 00:34:13,660
V-mail letters were sent from June 1942

406
00:34:13,661 --> 00:34:15,620
until September 1945.

407
00:34:18,300 --> 00:34:19,540
To ensure

408
00:34:19,541 --> 00:34:21,840
proper delivery of mail overseas,

409
00:34:22,360 --> 00:34:23,320
U.S. military

410
00:34:23,321 --> 00:34:25,540
post offices did not have names.

411
00:34:26,580 --> 00:34:27,440
They had numbers.

412
00:34:29,440 --> 00:34:31,380
Also, mailing addresses used

413
00:34:31,381 --> 00:34:33,140
by service personnel contained

414
00:34:33,141 --> 00:34:34,480
their serial numbers.

415
00:34:35,420 --> 00:34:40,728
Inspired by the efficiency of using a number system, in 1943 the U.S.

416
00:34:40,729 --> 00:34:42,420
Post Office Department

417
00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:50,020
developed postal zone coding for use of the country's 124 largest post offices.

418
00:34:50,021 --> 00:34:57,020
A one or two digit zone designation corresponded to the post office station from which letter

419
00:34:58,040 --> 00:35:04,040
carriers emanated. The result was fewer mail delivery errors.

420
00:35:04,041 --> 00:35:17,140
An improved system of coding mail which assigned each address in the United States a specific

421
00:35:17,141 --> 00:35:25,320
five-digit code was introduced in 1963.

422
00:35:25,321 --> 00:35:30,800
The ZIP or Zoning Improvement Plan code tells the post office in which of ten US regions

423
00:35:30,801 --> 00:35:37,980
and addresses located, as well as the major city or large area of population, and the

424
00:35:37,981 --> 00:35:42,740
local post office destination.

425
00:35:42,741 --> 00:35:45,516
The post office used the animated character Mr.

426
00:35:45,517 --> 00:35:47,760
Zip in a promotional blitz for the new

427
00:35:47,761 --> 00:35:52,200
system.

428
00:35:52,201 --> 00:35:57,880
In 1983, the Zip Code was amended with the addition of four numbers, which could pinpoint

429
00:35:57,881 --> 00:36:04,880
an addressee down to, for example, a particular floor in an office building.

430
00:36:07,280 --> 00:36:13,280
The speed and accuracy of mail delivery were greatly improved by use of the zip code.

431
00:36:13,281 --> 00:36:19,280
But for a postal system desperately in need of change, disaster was on the horizon, and

432
00:36:19,281 --> 00:36:25,880
eventually, rebirth.

433
00:36:25,881 --> 00:36:32,880
We now return to mail delivery on Modern Marbles.

434
00:36:32,881 --> 00:36:38,100
Years of financial neglect and management inefficiency, combined with outmoded facilities

435
00:36:38,101 --> 00:36:45,140
and low wages for workers, caught up with the post office in the mid-1960s.

436
00:36:45,141 --> 00:36:49,780
What happened was the total collapse of the Postal Service, and most people now don't

437
00:36:49,781 --> 00:36:51,320
know that.

438
00:36:51,321 --> 00:36:56,560
The Chicago Post Office, our largest post office, literally failed to process mail.

439
00:36:56,561 --> 00:37:03,940
Literally millions of pieces of mail were left stranded because the system was overloaded and could not process the mail.

440
00:37:04,740 --> 00:37:09,900
And at that point, the Postal Service itself had to say, we're in a race with catastrophe unless we change.

441
00:37:11,280 --> 00:37:18,680
A basic reorganization of the Post Office Department was proposed by Postmaster General Winton Blunt in 1969.

442
00:37:18,681 --> 00:37:27,680
It was approved and signed into law by President Richard Nixon on July 1st, 1971.

443
00:37:27,681 --> 00:37:36,560
It took a politically bloated system where patronage was rampant, where pay for letter

444
00:37:36,561 --> 00:37:44,120
carriers and clerks was deplorable, where mail volumes were astronomical but the ability

445
00:37:44,121 --> 00:37:47,316
to process the mail was ridiculously poor.

446
00:37:47,317 --> 00:37:51,120
It transformed all that into a much more efficient

447
00:37:51,400 --> 00:37:53,934
system that was much more humane.

448
00:37:53,935 --> 00:37:58,080
With Nixon's signature, the old post office department

449
00:37:58,081 --> 00:38:04,540
ceased to exist, and the new United States Postal Service officially began operations

450
00:38:04,541 --> 00:38:09,886
as a private government corporation, operated not under the authority of the U.S.

451
00:38:09,887 --> 00:38:10,587
Congress,

452
00:38:10,588 --> 00:38:15,760
but of a board of governors and postal service executives.

453
00:38:15,761 --> 00:38:24,760
Also authorization was given for collective bargaining with postal employee unions.

454
00:38:26,040 --> 00:38:31,500
In the Old West eager and well-equipped private express companies like Wells Fargo

455
00:38:31,501 --> 00:38:37,260
provided a serious direct challenge to the U.S. Post Office.

456
00:38:37,261 --> 00:38:43,140
Today, the modern U.S. Postal Service is once again being seriously challenged

457
00:38:43,141 --> 00:38:51,540
by a private industry that looks to have staked its claim to the global business arena.

458
00:38:51,541 --> 00:38:55,500
Companies such as Federal Express,

459
00:38:55,501 --> 00:38:58,380
DHL,

460
00:38:58,381 --> 00:39:02,820
and the world's largest United Parcel Service continue to dominate in the

461
00:39:02,821 --> 00:39:06,140
market for urgent delivery of mail and packages

462
00:39:06,141 --> 00:39:11,900
domestically and worldwide.

463
00:39:11,901 --> 00:39:22,900
Urgent delivery refers to rapid, often overnight, guaranteed delivery of an item.

464
00:39:22,901 --> 00:39:29,140
UPS alone transports more than three billion parcels and documents annually in the U.S.

465
00:39:29,141 --> 00:39:36,140
and to over 200 other countries, utilizing fleets of 147,000 motor vehicles and over

466
00:39:36,460 --> 00:39:43,460
500 aircraft. That makes UPS the largest airline in the world.

467
00:39:46,540 --> 00:39:47,749
To compete, the U.S.

468
00:39:47,750 --> 00:39:52,040
Postal Service has, in recent years, introduced its own urgent domestic

469
00:39:52,041 --> 00:39:58,380
and international delivery services, such as Priority Mail, domestic two-day delivery,

470
00:39:58,381 --> 00:40:05,380
mail, guaranteed domestic overnight delivery, and a guaranteed international service called

471
00:40:05,500 --> 00:40:07,760
Global Priority Mail.

472
00:40:07,761 --> 00:40:14,760
Competition has helped to redefine the Postal Service by waking us up to the need to change.

473
00:40:17,380 --> 00:40:21,842
There's nothing like a fit competitor to get yourself fit.

474
00:40:21,843 --> 00:40:24,380
And so competition is challenging

